## Purpose

Enforce recipient consent/capability checks on every relay first-contact delivery path before durable routing.

## Work

- Require valid recipient-issued authority in production before any durable first-contact enqueue. Prefer one backend admission operation (B261 `admit_contact_delivery` shape) rather than handler-only checks before `R.send`.
- Close all three live bypasses: `handle_send`/`R.send`, `handle_send_all`/`R.send_all`, and `handle_forward` → local `R.send`. Source-relay authentication is not recipient consent.
- Preserve Ed25519 sender authentication and alias binding; do not treat sender authentication as recipient consent.
- Apply identical validation to HTTP, WebSocket, connector outbox/sync, direct relay CLI, retries, and any batch/room-adjacent DM path. Connector inbound policy remains defence in depth only (post-accept).
- Secondary effects only on first accepted admission: `stats_note_message`, `Relay_ws_server.push_dm`, short-queue, observers. Note: `handle_send` currently treats `` `Ok `` and `` `Duplicate `` alike for pushes — fix for contact delivery.
- Rejected grants must never content-DLQ on either backend (InMemory local-send currently does for unknown/dead; Sqlite does not).
- Bind grants to intended recipient/host and sender; reject guessed, leaked-but-bound-elsewhere, expired, revoked, replayed, malformed, wrong-version, and downgraded authority.
- Enumeration-resistant errors; rate/size limits after admission only.
- Design input: `.collab/research/2026-07-22-b261-private-reachability-persistence-reconciliation.md`.

## Acceptance evidence

- An authenticated but unauthorised relay peer cannot deliver by knowing or guessing a private alias.
- Deliberate route/capability sharing permits only the intended scope.
- Revocation/expiry/replay take effect under concurrent requests and across restarts.
- Route and backend parity tests prove no bypass path remains across `/send`, `/send_all`, and `/forward`.

## Incremental handler-integration gaps and TDD order

B263 proves only backend grant lifecycle/atomic admit. B265 must separately prove handler and delivery-surface security. Required incremental groups:

1. Legacy authenticated `/send` to a private recipient rejects before `R.send`; inbox, dead letter, stats, WebSocket, short queue and observer effects remain empty.
2. Extract/use an Accepted-only DM side-effect seam. Current `handle_send` pushes WebSocket/short-queue/observer effects for both `` `Ok `` and `` `Duplicate ``; contact duplicate must not push or recount.
3. Add `POST /contact/v1/deliver`: valid grant delivers exactly once; duplicate produces no second effects; rejection is uniform and zero-side-effect; tokenless development fails closed.
4. `/send_all` excludes private recipients before enqueue and per-recipient effects.
5. Valid source-relay authentication on `/forward` is not recipient consent; unauthorised private forwarding produces no local enqueue/stats/DLQ. v1 cross-relay contact remains fail-closed.
6. Connector classifies uniform contact unauthorised as permanent and never retries through legacy `/send`.
7. A room roster/presentation alias does not open private DM reachability; room send semantics otherwise remain intact.
8. Add route/auth contract tests for contact delivery and owner management.

Primary proposed suite: `ocaml/test/test_relay_contact_delivery_handlers.ml`, using `Relay_test_support_real.with_server`. Reuse B263 ops only as fixtures; do not duplicate lifecycle assertions. Preserve B098 DATA/approval boundary. Exact seam map was produced during goal a8702492538a.

## Independent review findings requiring disposition

`handle_contact_deliver` must collapse all post-JSON-parse failures—missing/malformed fields, unsupported protocol, secret decode/length, sender identity resolution, and backend rejection—to one identical `401 contact_unauthorised` status/code/body. Keep differentiated diagnostics internal, bounded, and redacted. Add table-driven HTTP tests proving response equality and zero side effects.

## Final verified evidence

- Clean local-master branch merged as `ed1da828` (feature tip `ae03f950`); contaminated stale-origin branch was not merged.
- Exact post-review gates: delivery handlers 37/37; contact grants 36/36; private discovery 24/24; relay 54/54; connector 71/71; PoW 18/18; remote broker 37/37; auth matrix 45/45; forwarder 15/15; peer relay 11/11; client hints 10/10.
- Signed production tests prove byte-identical 401 `contact_unauthorised` across private `/send`, valid peer `/forward`, contact protocol/auth/grant denials, including unresolved sender.
- Signed production registration proves success/key binding and alias-substitution refusal.
- Contact admission returns delivery alias atomically; accepted inbox/WS wake effects occur exactly once; duplicate/rejected paths produce no inbox/stats/DLQ/push effect.
- Confidential transport requires native TLS or explicit `C2C_RELAY_TRUST_FORWARDED_PROTO` opt-in plus https; arbitrary forwarded header alone is refused.
- Independent exact-tip rereview PASS: no blocker/major.